  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1124 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nTonight through Friday Night...\n\nA warm front will lift through eastern Nebraska and western Iowa \ntonight, dragging warm moist air northward. Showers and a few \nthunderstorms will be possible north of this boundary overnight, \nprimarily over far northeast Nebraska into western Iowa. With \nmoisture pooling over the region, there remains at least some \npotential for fog to redevelop tonight, primarily over northeast \nNebraska. However, this could very well be limited by southeasterly \nsurface winds blowing at 5-15 mph. \n\nThe good news is, most of Friday looks to be fairly dry, with \ntemperatures rising well into the 80s and lower 90s across the \nregion. However, with the warming temperatures comes increased risk \nfor strong to severe storms. By mid afternoon, 2000-3000 J/kg of \nCAPE will settle into the mid Missouri Valley, ahead of a cold front \nsweeping in from the west. Deep layer shear still doesn't look that \nimpressive, at around 20-30 kts, but low level curvature in \nhodographs is quite notable. Large scale forcing for ascent still \nappears to be lacking, with the upper level short wave not expected \nto arrive until late Friday night, but any remnant MCV from previous \novernight convection could provide some lift for storm development. \nIf surface based storms are able to develop, the main threats would \nbe damaging wind gusts and tornadoes. \n\nScattered shower and storm chances will continue through Friday \nnight, as a LLJ ramps up ahead of the cold front. While the bulk of \nthe severe threat should end shortly after sunset, a strong elevated \nstorm or two, capable for gusty winds and hail, can't be ruled out. \n\nSaturday and Beyond...\n\nOff and on precipitation chances will continue through the middle of \nnext week, as a series of shortwaves approach from the west ahead of \nthe next upper level trough. The best chance for rain being Saturday \nnight into Sunday over far southeast Nebraska and southwest Iowa. \n\nCooler temperatures can be expected behind the front for the weekend \nand into next week. Highs on Saturday will drop back to the 70s and \n80s. By Sunday, most locations won't make it out of the 60s. Crisp, \ncooler fall temperatures continue through at least Wednesday, before \na ridge develops out west, and brings slightly warmer air back into \nthe central plains.",
